now i want to bring in christina raffini  who has been doing terrific work here on  the border for more than a week now uh  tell us about what you've seen and how  what we're looking at now is different  one of the things none of this was here  when we got here we got here the day  after the russian bombardment and there  was absolutely nothing here there was  one older woman a polish woman in a shop  giving out tea just out of her out of  her own pocket to people there was no  support system and there was just a  flood of people coming over the border  same is what you're seeing now lots of  women and children on their own who've  been walking for miles it is absolutely  freezing out here i don't have to tell  you that and every time i see them i  just can't think about you know how  these little kids have been out here in  the cold so long but the next day we  came back and people were putting up  tents they were people giving out cell  phones there were people giving out  meals and the response we've seen from  from the polish people and from people  all over europe we were at a bus station  where people had driven overnight from  germany and they were holding up signs  with different destinations all over  germany offering housing offering jobs  one man said it was it was some of the  worst days of his family's life getting  out of ukraine but when they got here  almost all their problems were over  because almost everything they needed  was given to them upon arrival now the  other thing i have noticed is it does  come in waves the first day people were  looking more and more just exhausted but  lately i think for the first time we're  starting to see real fear on people's  faces because so many people here in  poland didn't believe it could get to  this and the same for the ukrainians  christina thank you very much we  appreciate your coverage  and gail and nate as i send it back to  you it's important to remember that the  people here have so much need much of it  is provided for but after walking for  miles for hours sometimes for days i'll  say that what people want most is often  just a chair to sit down there was one  woman i talked to who had been on her  feet for 15 hours straight with her two  children before she finally found relief  and safety here in poland  yeah you know tony you and christina  touched on it i can't imagine what it's  like in those  in that circumstance under the best of  conditions but when you talk about the  freezing cold how cold is it there i  mean it just looks mixed it is add this  weather on top of what you're dealing  with  yeah it is bitterly  biting cold and it is windy it has been  spitting snow  all day  parents here hurry to feed their kids  the hot soup that's provided because it  too will go cold so the elements are yet  another thing to battle here in poland  well you got me with the dinosaur hat  and barbie because we can all relate to  that i don't care where you live you  know the universal symbol of what that  means to children thank you very much  tony
 

